WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:13.180
Hello and welcome to Al Dente Creepypasta,

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:17.640
horror stories cooked to perfection with a splash of red sauce.

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:22.420
My name is CZ and I will be your server this episode.

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:27.500
This story is about a swan song from one of YouTube's most promising young stars

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:29.800
taken from us too soon.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.140
Her name: Christina Grimmie.

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:33.740
Let's dig right in.

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:39.540
I've always been strangely fascinated by tragedy.

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:43.900
Now, don't get me wrong, it's not like I'm happy that terrible things happen.

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:47.140
I certainly don't encourage terrorism or anything, but

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:49.960
I have spent many nights on the weird side of YouTube,

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:55.240
watching 9/11 videos or watching houses get swept away by Hurricane Katrina.

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:59.400
I think we all have a small tendency for our eyes to be drawn to the morbid.

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:03.400
Just look how drivers stop to stare at the wreckage during a highway traffic accident

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:06.220
and in turn, slow down the traffic themselves.

00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:11.220
I also think that our curiosity with such things as a culture is related to our love for ghost towns.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:15.920
I always think it's amazing when a place that once had so much buzz and attention

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:18.020
becomes barren and empty so quickly.

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:21.560
Sometimes, tragedy and ghost towns can go hand in hand.

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:23.900
Just look at Pompeii or Chernobyl.

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:25.540
One night in late 2016,

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:27.420
I found a similar example.

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:30.380
A virtual ghost town, if you will.

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:33.500
While browsing YouTube at 3 in the morning before going to bed,

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:36.700
I stumbled upon a video from Christina Grimmie.

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:40.040
Christina was a YouTube singer who was tragically shot and killed

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:42.580
just after her concert in Orlando back in June.

00:01:42.580 --> 00:01:46.680
This was a truly sad and scary time for her fans and the community.

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:51.200
Christina was just 22 years old and she was one of the first big YouTubers to pass away.

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:56.200
Since she posted so regularly, I imagine that fans were left with a much bigger hole in their lives

00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:58.320
as compared to other celebrity deaths.

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:00.220
As I clicked through some of her videos

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:01.620
and read some of the comments,

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:04.520
I could see that many of her fans saw her as a friend,

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:06.680
and were torn apart at her death.

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:10.260
She even used to refer to her fan-base as her friend-base.

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:13.120
I came to think that I would have been friends with her too.

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:16.340
I hadn't heard about Christina until after her untimely death,

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:18.520
but in looking at some of her other content,

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:20.440
I started to see what the big deal was.

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:23.240
She was a beautiful girl with a beautiful voice.

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:25.600
She was a self-proclaimed Legend of Zelda nerd

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:27.740
and she even had a Death Note tattoo.

00:02:27.740 --> 00:02:30.300
I imagine we would have gotten along very well

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:31.360
had we known each other.

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:33.520
Naturally, I did quite a bit of digging.

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:35.780
Watching videos and reading comments.

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:38.120
There was a lot of pain carried on that page,

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:41.460
but in a way her channel was a lot like a ghost town.

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:44.920
Sure, there are still occasional posts from her manager,

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:48.420
including a memorial video and some prerecorded music videos,

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:51.000
but for the most part, it was a ghost channel.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:53.260
In many ways it served as a memorial,

00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:56.320
with people leaving their condolences on her discussion page.

00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:59.820
Most of the comments were fangirls talking about how much they miss her

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:02.860
with the occasional "why couldn't it have been Keemstar?" comment.

00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:05.900
But by far, the most interesting thing to me is

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:08.460
looking at those videos uploaded right before her death.

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:11.380
some of her final recorded moments on camera,

00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:13.880
and seeing people's reactions now that she's gone.

00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:15.840
I don't really know what it is.

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:18.880
I feel bad for her and her fans, but

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:21.820
These videos just captivate my attention.

00:03:21.820 --> 00:03:26.100
This led me to start looking at some of the tribute videos that have been made in her honor,

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:30.620
and it's here that I stumbled upon a playlist called "Christina Grimmie Unlisted".

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:34.200
So, I guess that Christina got her start by doing mostly covers

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:37.000
and some of them have since been struck down because of copyright

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:39.480
while others were switched to private or unlisted.

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:43.720
In fact, most of the videos on this playlist couldn't be watched because

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:44.940
they were changed to private.

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:46.660
But the rest of them were just unlisted.

00:03:46.660 --> 00:03:51.260
But what caught my attention was one unlisted video titled "FINAL GOODBYE."

00:03:51.940 --> 00:03:55.040
With a thumbnail of Christina waving at the camera.

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:58.340
The video was about 5 minutes long as I recall

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:02.900
and of course, I clicked on it to see what exactly this final goodbye video was.

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:06.760
Less than one second into the video, I was shocked with what I saw.

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:11.420
It was a video of Christina in her room, only...

00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:14.660
her face was covered in what looked like blood

00:04:14.660 --> 00:04:17.420
and she was crying her eyes out as she talked.

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:19.860
It looked like she had been sobbing for hours.

00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:22.180
She was holding the camera and recording herself

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:24.780
and it was basically just shaking all over the place.

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:29.360
My first thought was that this was some kind of Halloween or April fools prank that she had done

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:31.600
but I looked at the date of the video

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:33.920
and it said it was uploaded today,

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:35.360
which was obviously impossible.

00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:37.260
I was in complete shock.

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:42.800
It had only a couple hundred views and started with her barely able to get any words out through all the crying.

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:44.980
I can't remember all of her exact words

00:04:44.980 --> 00:04:47.600
but she started talking in typical vlog format

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:50.080
save for the fact that she was sobbing through all of it.

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:51.380
"Hey guys."

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:53.280
"It's Christina."

00:04:54.020 --> 00:04:58.500
"I... just wanted to say thank you for

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:01.860
all the love and support that you've given me."

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:03.540
"Over the last several months."

00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:08.140
"And... I'm sorry that I had to leave you like this."

00:05:08.140 --> 00:05:11.560
She was coughing and choking on her own words as she spoke.

00:05:11.740 --> 00:05:13.660
"I want you all to be strong."

00:05:13.660 --> 00:05:17.020
"I wish I could stay here with every last one of you."

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:21.300
"Thank you guys so much for being there and supporting me along the way."

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:24.340
"You guys were literally the biggest part of me."

00:05:24.340 --> 00:05:29.100
"It was an incredible journey, and I'm so sorry that it had to end this way."

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:33.800
"I loved going on tour so much because I got to see all of your smiling faces."

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:37.480
"And no one can take that away from me."

00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:38.680
"No matter what."

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:43.520
"I just wish that I had gotten one final chance to say goodbye."

00:05:45.500 --> 00:05:47.020
I guess I wasn't alone,

00:05:47.020 --> 00:05:50.180
because when I scrolled down to the comments they were pouring in.

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:52.020
Some of them were as shocked as I was

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:53.760
wondering how this was even possible

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:55.680
and trying to analyze the situation.

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:59.760
Others were just overwhelmingly thankful for her coming back to give them closure.

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:03.160
It seemed like all of the comments that I saw were positive ones.

00:06:03.160 --> 00:06:06.980
I guess because you'd have to be a fan of hers to be seeking out the video.

00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:08.760
Before I got to the end of the video,

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:11.040
it stopped playing on its own and I got the familiar

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:13.160
"an unknown error has occured" message.

00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:17.300
I tried refreshing the page, but it told me that the user had removed the video.

00:06:17.300 --> 00:06:20.060
I frantically went to her channel to see if it could be found, but

00:06:20.060 --> 00:06:21.340
it was nowhere.

00:06:21.340 --> 00:06:24.280
I searched YouTube to see if anyone has reuploaded it, but

00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:25.380
it couldn't be found.

00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:28.900
I even tried to find that "Christina Grimmie Unlisted" playlist,

00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:30.760
but it seemed like that was gone too.

00:06:30.760 --> 00:06:33.320
I was trying to process what had just happened.

00:06:33.420 --> 00:06:35.660
Had I dozed off and imagined that?

00:06:35.660 --> 00:06:37.300
I certainly didn't think so.

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:39.100
Everything seemed so real.

00:06:39.100 --> 00:06:42.900
It was so vivid that I still can't get her face in that video out of my head.

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:45.460
I was starting to think I had imagined it somehow,

00:06:45.460 --> 00:06:48.900
until I went to her channel and looked at the discussion comments.

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:51.540
Several comments had just come in, saying things like,

00:06:51.540 --> 00:06:53.760
"Thank you. Thank you for everything."

00:06:55.060 --> 00:06:55.560
Now...

00:06:56.820 --> 00:06:57.320
I'm...

00:06:57.620 --> 00:06:59.140
I'm not really sure...

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:07.700
Thanks for tuning in to this episode of Al Dente Creepypasta.

00:07:07.700 --> 00:07:11.880
Christina may be gone, but you can always go visit her YouTube channel

00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:13.400
where her music lives on.

00:07:13.700 --> 00:07:14.980
Speaking of music,

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:16.760
Go check out OST Maker,

00:07:16.760 --> 00:07:20.460
who put together the beautiful tribute that you heard in this video.

00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:23.640
Your bill this time came out to $64,

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:28.540
but if you like this video right now I'll waive that fee and give you everything you just listened to

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:30.240
absolutely free.

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:34.220
Remember to subscribe to CZsWorld for new horrors every other week,

00:07:34.220 --> 00:07:36.240
and I'll see you in the next one.

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:39.080
Assuming, we both survive.

